app/node_modules/
app/dist/
app/release/
app/build/core/anchorage-core
core/bin/

*.log
.DS_Store

# Design-parity working directories.
#
# `docs/design-qa/final-actual` IS committed: the visual review attestation binds the SHA-256
# of each of those 24 captures, so the gate cannot be verified without them. The capture is
# reproducible (tools/capture-design-parity.mjs freezes the clock and animation phase), so
# they are stable rather than churning on every run. Everything below is regenerated on
# demand and is large — the comparison sheets alone are 15 MB.
docs/design-qa/comparisons/
docs/design-qa/actual/
docs/design-qa/invalid/

# Every generated artefact, including the release evidence bundle that used to be committed.
#
# artifacts/host-candidate/ is the reason. Those captures are of a REAL Docker daemon — this
# machine's own containers, images and published ports — taken to prove the app works against
# something other than fixtures. They are evidence, they are not publishable, and the difference
# was not visible from the directory name. The JSON under artifacts/docker/ names the same
# projects. artifacts/design/ was already ignored for size.
#
# Nothing in `npm test` needs any of it: the checks that cite a digest generated into artifacts/
# run only when the file is present, and say so when it is not. A release is still judged against
# the bundle; the bundle is now produced and kept locally rather than versioned.
artifacts/

# Local environment. No .env exists today; this is here so one never lands by accident.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Key material must never be committed. No private key lives in this repository — signing
# reads the operator's own keyring through gpg-agent — and these patterns exist so one
# cannot land here by accident.
*.gpg
*.key
*.pem
secring.*
private-key*.asc
!anchorage-signing-key.asc

# Signed release output. The AppImage and its signature are published, not versioned.
app/release/

# The design handoff. A complete 21-screen design document, kept on the machines doing design
# work rather than in the published repository. Two committed things stand in for it: the
# colour block, extracted into app/scripts/handoff-tokens.json by tools/extract-handoff-tokens.mjs
# and asserted by the theme gate, and the SHA-256 in design-qa.md, which stays meaningful to
# anyone holding the file. The checks that read the comp itself skip out loud when it is absent.
docs/design_handoff_anchorage/

# The parity baseline, for the same reason: these 21 PNGs are renders OF the comp, and they show
# it more plainly than the HTML does — every screen, including the eight the app does not ship.
# They stay on the machines that regenerate them (tools/capture-design-reference.mjs writes them,
# tools/measure-design-parity.mjs reads them); neither runs in `npm test`. The captures of
# Anchorage itself under docs/design-qa/final-actual/ are ours and stay tracked.
docs/design-qa/reference/
